WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.710
Picture a café's old order book.

2
00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:10.927
For every order, the barista writes the customer's name, phone number, drink and price on one line.

3
00:00:11.133 --> 00:00:14.063
It's quick, and on the first day it works perfectly.

4
00:00:14.267 --> 00:00:17.280
Then a regular changes her phone number.

5
00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:22.404
Her number sits on 200 lines, and someone has to change all of them.

6
00:00:22.404 --> 00:00:23.609
They change 199.

7
00:00:23.867 --> 00:00:27.172
In an exam, 199 out of 200 is a great score.

8
00:00:27.172 --> 00:00:32.281
In a book of facts, it means the book now says two different things.

9
00:00:32.281 --> 00:00:33.934
Which number is right?

10
00:00:33.934 --> 00:00:35.136
Nobody can tell.

11
00:00:35.367 --> 00:00:38.402
A database table can fall into the same trap.

12
00:00:38.402 --> 00:00:44.404
A table is like a spreadsheet: each row is one record, and each column one fact about it.

13
00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:52.219
A partner bank, another company that handles card payments for some of the same shops, sends a file of payments every night.

14
00:00:52.219 --> 00:00:57.627
The quickest way to load it is to keep it as it arrives, with every detail on every row.

15
00:00:57.833 --> 00:01:06.139
The mcc is the merchant category code: four digits the card companies give each shop, to say what kind of business it is.

16
00:01:06.333 --> 00:01:10.724
Volt Electronics is in category 5732, electronics shops.

17
00:01:10.724 --> 00:01:13.939
This file says so four times in ten rows.

18
00:01:13.939 --> 00:01:17.703
A year of files would say it about 50,000 times.

19
00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:26.099
So the table is the order book, a row is a line, and Volt's category is her phone number: one fact, copied onto many rows.

20
00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:32.623
Every copy is a chance to disagree, and this small file already disagrees with itself twice.

21
00:01:32.623 --> 00:01:33.929
Can you spot where?

22
00:01:33.929 --> 00:01:34.960
Pause and look.

23
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:41.734
Payment 17 spells Farid's surname with one d.

24
00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:48.645
Payment 18 spells it with two, and calls category 5732 Electronic stores, without the s.

25
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.451
So which spelling is right?

26
00:01:50.451 --> 00:01:54.424
The table can't tell you, because nothing keeps the copies equal.

27
00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.040
You might be thinking: so be careful.

28
00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:00.404
Fix the bad rows, and next time, change every copy.

29
00:02:00.404 --> 00:02:03.175
To be fair, that does fix today's mistake.

30
00:02:03.367 --> 00:02:06.543
But careful is what the barista was, 199 times.

31
00:02:06.543 --> 00:02:14.181
A fact stored on many rows must be changed on all of them, and missing one leaves the table contradicting itself.

32
00:02:14.181 --> 00:02:16.344
That's called an update anomaly.

33
00:02:16.344 --> 00:02:19.927
An anomaly is something wrong that the design allows.

34
00:02:20.133 --> 00:02:22.787
And care can't help with the other two.

35
00:02:22.787 --> 00:02:25.646
Category 5814 means fast food restaurants.

36
00:02:25.646 --> 00:02:27.823
Where would you write that down?

37
00:02:27.823 --> 00:02:32.247
Every row is a payment, and no fast food payment has arrived yet.

38
00:02:32.247 --> 00:02:33.948
That's an insert anomaly.

39
00:02:34.167 --> 00:02:36.804
Now payment 21, the only Quick Bite row.

40
00:02:36.804 --> 00:02:39.970
Say it turns out to be fraud, and you delete it.

41
00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:43.531
The fact that Quick Bite is a restaurant goes with it.

42
00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:47.512
You came to remove one bad payment, and lost a restaurant.

43
00:02:47.512 --> 00:02:49.089
That's a delete anomaly.

44
00:02:49.333 --> 00:02:54.204
Three kinds of damage, and one cause: the same fact, stored on many rows.

45
00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:56.066
So what would the café do?

46
00:02:56.066 --> 00:02:59.377
Wouldn't it be nice to write her number down only once?

47
00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:03.952
Keep one card per customer, in a little box, with her name and number.

48
00:03:03.952 --> 00:03:06.688
Each line in the book then says customer 17.

49
00:03:06.688 --> 00:03:11.289
When her number changes, you change one card, and there's nothing to miss.

50
00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:15.252
Let's do the same with the table, starting with the categories.

51
00:03:15.252 --> 00:03:19.183
Someone still has to decide which spelling is true, but only once.

52
00:03:19.183 --> 00:03:22.101
The card companies' list says Electronics stores.

53
00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:26.434
Then each code and its description move to a table of their own.

54
00:03:26.434 --> 00:03:28.436
Ten rows of copies become four.

55
00:03:28.633 --> 00:03:33.632
From now on, there's one place to fix, and nowhere for a second spelling to live.

56
00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:37.743
The shops get their own table too: each shop and its code, once.

57
00:03:37.743 --> 00:03:41.685
The payments keep only the shop's name, which points at its row.

58
00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:46.474
Each shop's name now appears once in its table, and so does each code.

59
00:03:46.474 --> 00:03:52.943
A column like that is called a key: its value names exactly one row, so other rows can point at it.

60
00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:55.712
And the other two problems are gone.

61
00:03:55.712 --> 00:03:59.195
5814 gets its row today, with no payment needed.

62
00:03:59.195 --> 00:04:03.040
Delete payment 21, and Quick Bite keeps its category.

63
00:04:03.233 --> 00:04:07.924
Deciding which facts get a table of their own is called normalization.

64
00:04:07.924 --> 00:04:11.609
Farid's name needs the same cure: one row per customer.

65
00:04:11.767 --> 00:04:13.097
Splitting has a price.

66
00:04:13.097 --> 00:04:19.568
To print a payment with its category's description, the database now has to put three tables back together.

67
00:04:19.568 --> 00:04:22.411
That's called a join, and it's the next lesson.

68
00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:27.245
So split out the facts that belong to something else, like a shop or a category.

69
00:04:27.245 --> 00:04:29.973
Facts about the payment itself stay on its row.

70
00:04:30.167 --> 00:04:31.965
So, which spelling was right?

71
00:04:31.965 --> 00:04:36.244
Now the question can't come up again, because each fact has one home.

72
00:04:36.244 --> 00:04:41.515
Store each fact once, keyed by what decides it, and point at it from everywhere else.

73
00:04:41.967 --> 00:04:43.281
One quick check.

74
00:04:43.281 --> 00:04:48.620
The card companies rename category 7011, hotels, motels, resorts.

75
00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:52.317
In the old file, how many rows had to change?

76
00:04:52.317 --> 00:04:52.974
And now?

77
00:04:52.974 --> 00:04:54.288
Pause and think.

78
00:04:57.533 --> 00:05:02.531
Three in that file: payments 13, 16 and 22, and more every night.

79
00:05:02.531 --> 00:05:05.376
Now, one row in the categories table.
